Installation (Debian or Ubuntu with systemd, as root)

Use a clean server and clean VPS hosts that run nothing else: tun reconfigures them completely. On the server
it resets iptables on every start, takes over the routing tables 50, 51, 100, 200 and 300, turns on IP forwarding
and turns off IPv6; on every VPS it changes sshd_config, adds its key for root, turns on IP forwarding and resets
iptables (see VPS below).

curl -fsSL https://tunnelcontrol.app/install.sh | bash
                    # installs the latest release from GitHub into /opt/tunnelcontrol and creates tun.config
                    # from the example; another folder: ... | TUNNELCONTROL_DIR=/path bash
                    # then fill in CONFIG and TUNNELS in tun.config and run tun --install
git clone https://github.com/rdpex-com/tunnelcontrol.git /opt/tunnelcontrol && /opt/tunnelcontrol/tun --install
                    # the same from git; the first tun --install creates tun.config and the tun command, then stops
WEB_PANEL=0         # in tun.config before tun --install: console only, without the web panel and Node.js


Commands

tun --install       # installation and initialization: dependencies (with the web panel also Node.js 20+ and its packages),
                    # services, first VPS setup (asks for the VPS password); without tun.config it creates one from
                    # the example and stops. Running it again is safe: the config and data are not touched
tun --start         # start the tunnels
tun --restart       # restart the tunnels
tun --stop          # graceful stop of the tunnels
tun --kill          # immediate stop of the tunnels
tun --web-password  # set the web panel login and password (signs out every panel session)
tun --update        # update: from git (git pull) or, when installed with install.sh, from GitHub: compares VERSION
                    # and downloads the archive (UPDATE_URL) only for a newer version, then runs tun --install;
                    # only the code is replaced, tun.config, tun.server.setup.*, data and bak/ are not touched
tun --version       # installed version
tun --help          # this list of commands; tun without a command or with an unknown one prints it too
tun --status        # whether the tunnels run from the console or as the tun service, the service, the web panel,
                    # then Global Stats, Active Tunnels and System Information as in the web panel: every host
                    # with its tunnels (state, port, weight, speed, traffic), CPU, RAM, disk and host data
tun --monitor [N]   # the same, redrawn every N seconds (3 by default); keys: 1-9 turn the host with that number
                    # on or off (as tun --toggle), q quits
tun --toggle <IP>   # turn a host on or off, like its switch in Active Tunnels of the web panel: the tunnels of a
                    # turned off host leave the routing and the rotation; the last active host cannot be turned
                    # off; holds until the tunnels restart; without an address lists the hosts with their state


Settings

tun.config          # your config, created from tun.config.example; never overwritten by updates;
                    # tun does not start while it is identical to the example
tun.defaults        # default values of all settings with descriptions; do not edit it —
                    # to change a setting, add a line with it to tun.config, for example ROTATION_DELAY=30
tun.config.check    # config check; tun does not start if the config has anything but settings

TUNNELS in tun.config has one line per channel. Three lines per VPS are recommended for the smoothest
rotation: it reconnects one channel at a time, and the other channels of that VPS carry its traffic meanwhile.
DATA_PATH in tun.config is the data folder; when the path changes, the data moves on the next start.
Web panel settings are WEB_* in tun.defaults: address and ports, HTTPS, e-mail for push notifications,
certificate address and validity. Override them in tun.config, they apply when the panel restarts:
systemctl restart tun-web (certificate address and validity only when the certificate is created;
WEB_AUTH and WEB_PUSH_EMAIL at once, without a restart).


Logs

LOG_LEVEL=1         # in tun.config: 0 = errors and warnings; 1 (default) = plus key events: install, start and
                    # stop, VPS setup, update; 2 = plus all other messages; 3 = plus debug messages; applies
                    # when the tunnels restart (tun --restart), the panel request log on systemctl restart tun-web
/var/log/tun.log    # errors, warnings and key events at every level
/var/log/tun.verbose.log   # every message, from LOG_LEVEL=2
/var/log/tun.mon.*.log     # traffic monitor logs from LOG_LEVEL=2, its debug log from LOG_LEVEL=3
/var/log/tun.web.log       # web panel log; its request log from LOG_LEVEL=3
/etc/logrotate.d/tun       # added by tun --install: a log over 10 MB is rotated at the daily logrotate run,
                           # two compressed copies are kept
LOG_VPS_FILE        # log file on every VPS, /var/log/tun.log by default; LOG_VPS_FILE="/tmp/tun.log" leaves
                    # nothing behind after a VPS reboot; applies on the next VPS setup, for example tun --restart
The Logs tab of the web panel lists the main log and the other log files that exist. After the level is lowered,
it also warns how much the logs of the higher level take in /var/log, until they are deleted.


VPS

The VPS must be clean: tun manages its firewall. On every VPS setup (tunnel start, setup of a VPS
that went down) iptables is reset and incoming connections are closed except ping, SSH on ports 22
and PORT, and the PORT_RANGE range for the local server (PORT and PORT_RANGE come from TUNNELS).

A VPS serves one local server at a time. The server keeps a random session mark until it reboots
(/run/tunnelcontrol/session, copied to data/tun_session), and the VPS setup records it in /tmp/tun_busy on the
VPS. Another server, or a copy of this one, that tries to set up the VPS while its tunnels are up is refused
and touches nothing there. It marks that VPS busy (data/tun_host_<VPS IP>_busy), logs it, sends one push
notification and leaves the VPS out until the tunnels are restarted (tun --start, --restart, --install): no
retries, no rotation counting it, and its tunnels are not listed in tun --status or in the panel. The VPS becomes free when the tunnels of its server are
gone or the VPS reboots.

tun.server.setup.<VPS IP>   # optional file with your own commands for this VPS: open a port, an allow list,
                            # start your own scripts; runs as root on the VPS after the base firewall rules
                            # on every VPS setup. Unix line endings (LF) only. Updates do not touch it.
                            # Example line:
                            # iptables -I INPUT 1 -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT


Web panel

https://<server address>:8443
WEB_AUTH=1          # login with user and password (default); WEB_AUTH=0 in tun.config = no login
WEB_PUSH_EMAIL      # e-mail for push notifications; tun --install asks for it while it is the default
                    # root@localhost; Apple devices (iPhone, iPad, Safari on Mac) need a real address;
                    # change it in tun.config at any time, the panel uses it at once, devices stay subscribed
https://<server address>:8443/cert.pem   # panel certificate to install on a phone or a PC
?debug=1 / ?debug=0 in the panel address # turn debug mode on / off (remembered in the browser)


